in july of 1987 our mom filed four of us  kids in the car including John and Leigh  to take us on our annual summer vacation  in the wilderness of upstate New York  our parents have a small house up there  it's in the middle of nowhere about a  mile from the nearest neighbor to this  day cell phone reception does not exist  at that location as kids we spent our  time running out into the forest  creating our own adventures with no  adult supervision it was paradise we  spent our time playing in streams and  chasing frogs building forts all the  things that kids do and we wore mosquito  bites like a badge of honor because they  were the price we paid for absolute  freedom and it was an awesome experience  I consider us so lucky that we had the  opportunity to explore the natural world  at such a young age with no adult  supervision today 27 years later chris  and i both have kids in fact our  daughters are eight days apart we still  go to that same house in upstate New  York except now my mother our mother my  son mine too my son's grandmother is  deathly afraid of letting the grandkids  out of her sight why does my brother  have to remind her and I that at the  same age we too are running all over  those woods what has changed so  significantly in our family in less than  a generation and why do my kids now  experience a shrunken radius of play and  what does that change costing us in  terms of our kids character confidence  and creativity hi I'm Chris boggiano and  I'm John boggiano we're both parents  educators and lifelong learners it's two  dads who hope to raise happy and healthy  kids of our own we spent a lot of time  wondering what does that mean and what  can we actually do to make it happen  we've interacted with thousands of  students and parents educators and  innovators and today we want to share  with you some of what we found almost  everyone we've spoken to has expressed  the dismay  current state of American education and  almost all of their anxiety is related  to children's test scores while we don't  want to diminish diminish the importance  of test scores we think there's a  fundamentally overlooked factor of  success and that's a management of play  when we were kids the only toy in our  grandmother's house was a tub of Legos  that so far as I could tell had been  there since the beginning of time we  spent hundreds of hours playing with  those blocks throughout our childhood  because a tub of Legos that is an  invitation to imagine if play it fosters  children's social emotional and  intellectual skills by allowing them to  try out and master new things but even  today Legos have changed my son Julian  on several times his own body weight in  Legos  true however almost all of them look  like this they come in pre-scripted sets  with step-by-step instructions pre  imagined by somebody else he gets upset  at this Ninjago dragon loses even one  single piece where his two year old  cousin Olivia comes along and smashes it  on him or his sister I however get  excited when he builds this which is a  Star Destroyer made at a lego flat  plates and it's his imagination of what  it would look like on the inside and he  gets to play with this you see we  believe that the more play is a creation  of children's minds their own minds the  more that they are authors of their own  identity the more powerful it is and  it's regardless of whether they're the  screen writer or the scientist the ninja  warrior or the princess but when we say  play a lot of parents end up thinking  playdates but we believe that playdates  can beat a childhood what microwave  dinner is to food seemingly healthy  well-intentioned highly refined and it  turns out that when you look a little  closer they're totally devoid of any  nutritional value the subtle shift  towards structured play can strip away  the spontaneity creativity and curiosity  they're all natural elements of healthy  childhood development for the nba's in  the crowd try to think about a pack of  kids just running around outside of no  adult supervision that's like a life  internship it requires all the things  you pay money to come to Stanford to  learn strategy negotiations probably  even some interpersonal dynamics safety  and success these are fundamental to the  mindset of today's parents so let's  start with safety whether it's because  of the 24-hour cable news the internet  or Amber Alerts today's parents are  deathly afraid that there is a child  predator around every corner parents  have always wanted their kids to be safe  but today there's a pervasive anxiety  and it's affecting our kids we've tried  talking to parents about statistics  would show that we live in the safest  times on modern record however it  doesn't matter in my  self included every one of us would say  better safe than sorry so how do we  balance the benefits of imaginative play  with concern for our children safety  this is where the radius comes in rather  than a narrow path or a checklist  defining success let's imagine a wide  radius or a boundary and within that  boundary we should give our kids free  rein to imagine create and explore their  own worlds so let me be clear  imaginative play is not anarchy it does  not mean there are no rules it's a  healthy part of childhood development  I'm not asking you to run out of here  and find the nearest six-year-old and  give them some matches scissors and  superglue and let them see what he can  come up boundaries are the line between  safety and recklessness the boundary for  your ten-month-old might be the walls of  her pack and play and the boundary for  your ten-year-old might be the area  between your house and its best friend's  house all the way down the street  success the other fundamental factor  every parent wants their kids to be  successful however parents in society  today are increasingly defining success  as the narrow path that leads through  the elite college admissions process  some of this is related to the shrinking  family size our grandmother was one of  seven children our father was one of  four today one or two is more common the  benefits of fewer children are that more  parental time and energy can be spent on  each individual kit the downside the  downside is the paralyzing fear that  your perfect six-year-old won't get into  Stanford we hold their own kids hostage  to our own hope so much so that won't  let them risk failure get in trouble you  want to know a secret we spoke with the  folks in the Stanford admissions office  guess what they reject sixty-nine  percent of applicants with perfect s80  scores and you know what else do you  know what they told us they're looking  for in their perfect applicant  in their own words it's someone who has  found his or her own voice so what does  that it means that they're looking for  people who aren't relying on Stanford to  define who they are so how do we help  kids find their own voice imaginative  play on structured activities creative  freedom that's the key to building  character confidence and creativity as  parents I encourage you to think about  why boundaries rather than narrow roads  and precise checklists boundaries give  us permission to both keep kids safe and  allow them to become authors of their  own identity as a parent myself I would  love it if my kids could come to  Stanford if that's what they wanted to  do however I also realized that if I  push them too hard and predefined  success for them in too much of a  certain way I am likely to cause their  failure or hinder their chances of  getting it so my challenge as a parent  is to help my kids find their own voice  and our challenge as a society is to  give our kids the space to develop  within their own radius of play thank  you very much  you
 

